---
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Final Thoughts - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="final-thoughts">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				If there is one theme running through this entire training, it is this:{" "}
				<strong class="text-stone-800">AI is most useful when you treat it as leverage, not magic.</strong>{" "}
				It can help you think faster, write faster, research faster, and explore more options.
				But it does not remove the need for judgment. In most cases, it makes judgment more important.
			</p>

			<p>
				The people who get the most value from these tools are usually not the people with the
				most elaborate setup. They are the people who give clear context, break work into
				reasonable chunks, review output carefully, and keep improving their workflow over time.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Start simpler than you think">
				<p>
					Do not try to adopt every skill, MCP server, agent mode, and workflow pattern at once.
					Start with one or two high-value use cases, get reliable results, then layer on more.
					Complexity in your AI setup creates its own failure modes.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>The Work Is Still Yours</h3>

			<p>
				This training covered requirements, research, task breakdowns, development,
				documentation, and testing. Across all of those areas, the pattern is the same:
				the AI can accelerate the first draft, but the human still owns the direction,
				the review, and the final decision.
			</p>

			<p>
				That is why so much of effective AI usage looks a lot like good senior-level work.
				You write clearer briefs. You provide better context. You verify facts. You review
				output more rigorously. You document decisions so the next session starts from a
				better place. The leverage comes from doing those things well, not from avoiding them.
			</p>

			<h3>Prompt Like You Are Delegating</h3>

			<p>
				A vague prompt often produces fast, convincing, low-value output. A detailed prompt
				is closer to writing requirements for a teammate: what the goal is, why it matters,
				what constraints exist, what files or documents matter, and what good looks like.
			</p>

			<p>
				This is especially important because the AI is stateless. It does not carry forward
				yesterday's corrections, your team's unwritten rules, or the background sitting in
				your head. Every strong result starts with good context.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="example" title="The practical mental model">
				<p>
					Treat the AI like a capable but inexperienced teammate who joins for one task,
					works very quickly, and leaves with no memory when the session ends.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Review More, Not Less</h3>

			<p>
				AI changes where the time goes. You usually spend less time producing a first draft
				and more time reviewing, testing, and refining. That is not overhead. That is the job.
			</p>

			<p>
				The output may look polished even when it is wrong. Confidence and correctness are still
				separate things. So whether the AI is writing requirements, summarizing research,
				generating tests, or changing code, the standard stays the same: verify what matters,
				challenge assumptions, and only ship what you understand.
			</p>

			<h3>Build a Workflow That Fits You</h3>

			<p>
				You do not need a perfect system on day one. What you need is a repeatable workflow that
				makes your actual work better. Try a few patterns from this training. Keep the ones that
				save time without lowering quality. Drop or modify the ones that don't work for you.
			</p>

			<p>
				The goal is not to become someone who uses AI for everything. The goal is to become
				someone who knows when AI helps, when it hurts, and how to guide it productively.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Take this into the practical">
				<p>
					Start with a small task. Give the AI strong context. Keep the scope tight. Review
					the output like it came from a junior teammate. Then adjust your workflow based on
					what actually happened, not on the marketing around the tools.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
